## Code Quality Rules

- **Always write code in numbered, well-defined sections** consistent with the style of the surrounding file
- **Always double-check code correctness** before presenting it — logic, variable names, control flow
- **Always verify function I/O:** confirm that input types/structures and output types/structures match actual usage at every call site
- **Never fabricate variable operational definitions (categories, cutoffs, coding) from "standard-sounding" conventions.** Before writing Methods-section text describing covariate categories (BMI, physical activity, alcohol, education, income, periodontitis, etc.), OPEN the project's variable-definition script (typically `R/02_variable_definition.R`) and READ the exact `case_when()` / cut / factor levels. NHANES and other surveys do not have a single canonical scheme — the researcher may have coded BMI as 2-level (<30/≥30) or 3-level (<25/25–30/≥30), pacat/alc as 2-level dichotomies vs. 3-level, etc. Guessing propagates wrong descriptions into the manuscript.

