## Known R Pitfalls

- **Do not use `dplyr::coalesce`** — causes crashes. Use base R alternatives instead:
  ```r
  # Instead of coalesce(x, y):
  ifelse(is.na(x), y, x)
  # or
  x[is.na(x)] <- y[is.na(x)]
  ```

- **Closure scoping in `for` loops** — when creating functions inside a `for` loop, the loop variable is captured by reference. All iterations silently use the last value. Fix with an IIFE (Immediately Invoked Function Expression) + `force()`:
  ```r
  # WRONG — all shift_fn will use the final value of d and max_val:
  for (d in deltas) {
    shift_fn <- function(data, trt) pmin(data[[trt]] + d, max_val)
  }

  # CORRECT — each iteration gets its own copy:
  for (d in deltas) {
    shift_fn <- (function(dd, mx) {
      force(dd); force(mx)
      function(data, trt) pmin(data[[trt]] + dd, mx)
    })(d, max_val)
  }
  ```
  This applies to any pattern where closures are generated in loops: `lapply` with factory functions, custom shift/link functions for `lmtp`, callbacks, etc.

- **Duplicate column names in SuperLearner / lmtp** — if a variable list passed to `lmtp_tmle()` (e.g., `time_vary`, `baseline`) contains duplicate column names, `predict.SuperLearner` fails with the cryptic error `[.data.frame(newdata, , object$varNames): undefined columns selected`. The error gives no hint about duplicates. Common cause: building variable vectors by concatenation where a name already exists (e.g., `c(l_cols(6), "dep_cesd_t6")` when `l_cols()` already includes `dep_cesd`). Always check `duplicated()` on variable lists before passing to lmtp/SuperLearner.

- **Named vector propagation in arithmetic** — when a function returns a named scalar (e.g., `c(est = 0.297)`), arithmetic on it preserves the name. Then `c(stat = named_scalar)` produces `stat.est = 0.297` instead of `stat = 0.297`. Downstream `sapply(results, "[", "stat")` returns NA silently. Fix: use `unname()` at the top of any function that receives values from external sources and does arithmetic on them:
  ```r
  wald_test <- function(est1, se1, est2, se2) {
    est1 <- unname(est1); se1 <- unname(se1)
    est2 <- unname(est2); se2 <- unname(se2)
    diff <- est1 - est2
    # ...
  }
  ```

- **`svykm` on large NHANES (N>15,000): NEVER use `se = TRUE`** — it crashes R on memory with multi-cycle survey designs. This has caused repeated session failures. Two safe alternatives:
  - **For plots only** (no manual `$time`/`$surv` extraction): use `survfit()` with survey weights. This is fast and reliable:
    ```r
    km <- survfit(Surv(time, event) ~ group, data = df, weights = WTMECALL)
    plot(km, conf.int = FALSE, col = c("blue", "red"))
    ```
  - **For CIs**: use `svykm(se = FALSE)` + PSU bootstrap (1000 reps). Note that `se = FALSE` returns an `approxfun` closure, NOT a list — `$time`/`$surv` will be NULL. Use `plot.svykm()` directly or the bootstrap approach.
  - `svykm(se = TRUE)` is only safe on small datasets (N<5,000). On large NHANES it will segfault or exhaust memory.

- **Em-dash / Unicode in `cat()` on Windows R** — `cat("text — more text")` with UTF-8 em-dashes (U+2014) can crash the R parser on Windows, especially after many warnings have accumulated from packages like SuperLearner. The error is cryptic: `unexpected symbol in "...)ull"`. Fix: always use plain ASCII dashes (`-` or ` - `) in `cat()` strings on Windows. This also applies to other Unicode characters in diagnostic messages.

