## Debugging

When debugging code that previously worked, ALWAYS do a line-by-line comparison of the working version vs the failing version. Check:
- Variable names
- Function calls
- Data structures
- How formulas/models are constructed and used (hardcoded vs stored in variables)

Never assume the logic is identical — verify every detail.

## Reasoning About Prior Runs

When referencing prior run times, resource usage, or results to estimate future runs, ALWAYS verify what conditions the prior run actually used. A prior run may have used different settings (e.g., sequential vs parallel, different learner library, different N) than the current run. Do not carry over assumptions — check the log or code version that produced the prior result. Common mistakes:
- Assuming a prior run used parallelism when it didn't (or vice versa)
- Treating a prior run's wall time as a baseline without checking how many workers/cores were active
- Forgetting that a code fix (e.g., enabling parallel, adding HAL) was made AFTER the prior run completed

